      'SEBoK states the purpose of the decision management process, quoting ISO/IEC/IEEE 15288, as providing a structured, analytical framework for objectively identifying, characterising and evaluating a set of alternatives for a decision at any point in the life cycle and selecting the most beneficial course of action. Decisions need a formal process when they involve many stakeholders, competing objectives, substantial uncertainty and significant consequences. The trade study is the method systems engineers use most; it decomposes the decision into parts that experts can assess within their own expertise and then synthesises the parts, so the decision maker can work within human cognitive limits without oversimplifying the problem (R18 p.463).',
      'The process SEBoK describes is iterative and rests on four best practices: sound decision-analysis mathematics; one master decision model that is refined and reused through the life cycle; value-focused thinking (Keeney) to create better alternatives; and identifying uncertainty and assessing risk for each decision (R18 p.464). It begins with framing: describe the system baseline, boundaries and interfaces, the life-cycle stage, decision milestones, decision makers, stakeholders and resources, and write a decision problem statement in terms of the life cycle (R18 p.465). Your Week 1 boundary, Week 2 stakeholder map and Week 6 AROU register are exactly these inputs.',
