      'SEBoK asks subject-matter experts to discuss the uncertainty of the independent variables — the variables that drive one or more scores. Often an expert can give a lower, nominal and upper bound by assuming low, moderate and high performance. A Monte Carlo simulation then summarises the impact of these uncertainties and can identify the ones that matter most to the decision (R18 p.471). Decision analysis uses several forms of sensitivity analysis — line diagrams, tornado diagrams and waterfall diagrams — and several forms of uncertainty analysis, including Monte Carlo simulation, decision trees and influence diagrams; a line diagram is used to show sensitivity to the swing-weight judgement (R18 p.472).',
      'Two further activities turn analysis into a better decision. Mining the results usually reveals ways to modify an alternative to capture untapped value or reduce risk, which moves the team from alternative-focused to value-focused thinking. Then the team identifies the key trade-offs, uncertainties and risks and presents the recommendation as a clearly worded, actionable task list, in a report that preserves the rationale for future decisions (R18 p.472–473).',
      'Finally, SEBoK warns that cognitive bias can seriously distort decisions and that self-mitigation by the decision maker is not feasible for most biases. It names confirmation bias as the best known, and rankism, complacency and optimism as typical. Mitigations include an independent review body that is organisationally and financially independent of the programme, crew resource management, and Kahneman’s premortem, in which trusted experts are asked to argue the case against the decision before it is made (R18 p.473–474).',
